---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import cover from '../../assets/studio-shum-cover.jpg';

const preserved = [
  { value: '38', label: 'source routes, including every project and article' },
  { value: '25', label: 'project pages with their specifications and prices' },
  { value: '5', label: 'published articles kept at their original addresses' },
  { value: '466', label: 'media and font files restored and checksum-verified' },
  { value: '16', label: 'project videos carried over without re-encoding' },
];

const results = [
  { measure: 'Mobile Performance, PageSpeed', result: '96 / 100' },
  { measure: 'Desktop Performance, PageSpeed', result: '98 / 100' },
  { measure: 'Largest Contentful Paint, mobile', result: '2.7 s' },
  { measure: 'Accessibility audit', result: '100 / 100' },
  { measure: 'Legacy redirect and 410 checks', result: '105 / 105' },
];

const stack = [
  'Astro 7',
  'Node.js',
  'SQLite',
  'Sharp',
  'Typograf',
  'Three.js',
  'Playwright',
  'Nginx',
  'GitHub Actions',
  'Deploy-package verification',
  'Smoke tests',
  'Atomic deploy and rollback',
];
---

<CaseStudy
  pageTitle="studio-shum.ru — Alexander Zhiganov"
  title="studio-shum.ru"
  role="The studio site became unreachable for the audience it was built for. I led the move to infrastructure we control: every URL preserved, leads routed to the studio, and every release reversible."
>
  <section class="case-section case-section-full">
    <div class="case-content case-open">
      <figure class="case-figure">
        <Image
          src={cover}
          alt="studio-shum.ru homepage in production with the SHUM navigation and a multimedia installation hero"
          class="case-img"
          widths={[600, 1000, 1400, 1900]}
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 85vw"
        />
        <figcaption>
          The rebuilt homepage in production. The design, projects, and
          enquiry path stayed; the infrastructure underneath changed.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>The site was unreachable where it mattered.</h2>
    <div class="case-content">
      <p class="pull-quote">
        When studio-shum.ru was blocked in Russia, we started losing potential
        leads. I urgently rebuilt the website on infrastructure we could
        control.
      </p>
      <p>
        studio-shum.ru is not a portfolio decoration. It is where the studio
        explains its services, shows 25 projects, publishes articles, and
        receives enquiries. When Russian visitors could no longer reach it, the
        site stopped doing all four jobs at once.
      </p>
      <p>
        The old setup also sent enquiries through an embedded form on someone
        else's platform. The studio could not see how many people tried to
        write and failed. The rebuild had to solve availability, preserve the
        site, and put the lead path under the studio's control.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Faithful meant keeping everything that already worked.</h2>
    <div class="case-content">
      <p>
        This was not a redesign. The site was reconstructed from its live
        source, page by page, so the material the studio had built over years
        survived the move: project dossiers with specifications and prices,
        articles, team cards, brand typography, and the original media.
      </p>

      <ul class="inventory">
        {preserved.map((item) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{item.value}</strong>
            <span>{item.label}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <p>
        Only the foundation changed. Hosting, the enquiry form, deployment,
        caching, image delivery, and typographic normalization were replaced.
        The visible site stayed recognizable to the people who already knew it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>The site moved in one piece.</h2>
    <div class="case-content">
      <p>
        Every asset was restored locally, checksum-verified, and served from
        the new build instead of Squarespace or a raw GitHub URL. The original
        files were never re-encoded or overwritten. The 16 project videos were
        saved from the public streams as they were.
      </p>
      <p>
        URLs were handled as a product decision, not a cleanup. The 25 flat
        project addresses moved to <code>/projects/[slug]</code> with permanent
        301 redirects. <code>/home</code> redirects to the homepage. Eight tag
        routes and one empty category were removed with HTTP 410 Gone, a clear
        signal to search engines instead of redirect debt.
      </p>
      <p>
        The old site also depended on a Squarespace runtime for navigation,
        galleries, filters, consent, and video. All of it was rebuilt in owned
        code. Three.js appears once, on the branded 404 page, loaded lazily so
        it never touches normal page startup.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>A lead no longer depends on a third-party form.</h2>
    <div class="case-content">
      <p>
        The new form is part of the site. It posts to a small Node service on
        loopback, behind Nginx, and the service writes the enquiry into a
        SQLite outbox before delivery. Telegram is the notification, not the
        database. If the assistant is unavailable, the record waits in the
        queue and is retried instead of disappearing.
      </p>

      <ol class="lead-steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Native form</strong>
          <span>Validation, origin checks, rate limits, honeypot.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Node service</strong>
          <span>Loopback only, isolated behind Nginx.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>SQLite outbox</strong>
          <span>Recorded first, retried if delivery fails.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Telegram topic</strong>
          <span>Delivered to the lead topic, with email fallback.</span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Delivery goes to the studio's Telegram assistant. Duplicate submissions
        return the existing record instead of creating a second lead. Personal
        fields are purged from SQLite after 90 days, and the Telegram card is
        anonymized after 30. The public email address remains as a fallback.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>A release is a switch, not an upload.</h2>
    <div class="case-content">
      <p>
        The first version of deployment copied files into the live directory. A
        failed transfer could leave the site half-updated, and there was no
        quick way back. The new path builds a complete release, verifies it,
        uploads it beside the current one, and moves a symlink.
      </p>

      <ol class="release">
        <li>
          <strong>Build</strong>
          <span>Static HTML, normalized typography, responsive WebP.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Check</strong>
          <span>Tests, internal links, media references, deploy package.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Package</strong>
          <span>Release archive with a SHA-256 manifest, media hardlinked.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Switch</strong>
          <span>The current symlink moves to the new release in one step.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Verify</strong>
          <span>Smoke tests on live routes, automatic rollback on failure.</span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        If a smoke test fails, the symlink returns to the previous release
        automatically. Five releases are retained, and the media bank is
        hardlinked between them, so a 476 MiB video library is not copied five
        times. The same pipeline runs every check on <code>dev</code> and only
        deploys from <code>main</code>. Publishing stopped being an event that
        depended on someone's laptop.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Speed was part of the migration.</h2>
    <div class="case-content">
      <p>
        The homepage was carrying oversized images and nine blocking CSS
        requests. Hero images were generated from 4x masters into responsive
        WebP at 750, 1080, 1920, and 2560 pixels, cutting the mobile LCP image
        weight by about 3.18 MB, a 95.8% reduction. CSS requests dropped from 9
        to 3, fonts load with <code>font-display: swap</code>, and hashed assets
        and media are cached for a year as immutable.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same pass normalized heading levels across all 38 pages and
        brought the accessibility audit to 100/100.
      </p>

      <table class="results">
        <caption>Measured on the live site on 18 October 2026.</caption>
        <tbody>
          {results.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{row.measure}</th>
              <td>{row.result}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p class="case-note">
        These are dated measurements, not a permanent promise. PageSpeed moves
        with network, device, and third-party scripts. The work was to remove
        the causes, not to chase a number.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>The move also had to keep the site findable.</h2>
    <div class="case-content">
      <p>
        Redirects were only one part. The rebuild shipped structured data for
        the organization, the website, project and publication collections, and
        individual articles. It also added page and video sitemaps, canonical
        URLs, and a stable route map that does not change when content is
        updated.
      </p>
      <p>
        All 105 legacy redirect and 410 checks pass on production. Google Search
        Console and Yandex Webmaster are connected with active sitemaps. The
        site also publishes <code>llms.txt</code> and machine-readable content
        signals, so agents can read the studio's public material without
        scraping the layout.
      </p>
      <p>
        Search visibility is not something I can promise to preserve in a
        screenshot. It is something I can make measurable, then watch.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>What this proves.</h2>
    <div class="case-content">
      <p>
        I did not need a new design to make the site work. I needed a foundation
        the studio could own. I led the rebuild: I made the product, content,
        and infrastructure decisions, and AI agents wrote and shipped the code.
        The result is a site that serves its own pages, records its own leads,
        and can return to the previous release in seconds.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the part I would repeat on any project: define the constraint,
        keep what already works, and make the production path boring. The live
        site is at
        <a
          href="https://studio-shum.ru"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          aria-label="studio-shum.ru live site (opens in a new tab)"
          >studio-shum.ru <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Stack</h2>
    <div class="case-content">
      <ul class="stack-list">
        {stack.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  :global(.case-body .case-content.case-open) {
    max-width: none;
  }

  .inventory {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    margin: 0 0 1.4em;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    background: var(--bg-surface);
  }

  .inventory li {
    display: grid;
    gap: 4px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 18px 20px;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .inventory li:nth-child(2n) {
    border-right: 0;
  }

  .inventory li:last-child {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    border-right: 0;
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  .inventory strong {
    font-size: var(--type-lead);
    font-weight: 650;
    line-height: 1;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .inventory span {
    font-size: var(--type-small);
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--mute);
  }

  .lead-steps {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1px;
    margin: 0 0 1.4em;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    background: var(--rule-strong);
  }

  .lead-steps li {
    display: grid;
    align-content: start;
    gap: 6px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 16px 18px;
    background: var(--bg-surface);
  }

  .lead-steps strong {
    font-size: var(--type-body);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .lead-steps span {
    font-size: var(--type-small);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--mute);
  }

  .release {
    margin: 0 0 1.4em;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  }

  .release li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(88px, 0.34fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 4px 20px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 12px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .release strong {
    font-size: var(--type-body);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }

  .release span {
    font-size: var(--type-small);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--mute);
    padding-top: 2px;
  }

  .results {
    width: 100%;
    margin: 0 0 1em;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--type-small);
  }

  .results caption {
    padding-bottom: 10px;
    text-align: left;
    color: var(--mute);
  }

  .results th,
  .results td {
    padding: 11px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    vertical-align: baseline;
  }

  .results tbody tr:first-child th,
  .results tbody tr:first-child td {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  }

  .results th {
    font-weight: 500;
    text-align: left;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .results td {
    text-align: right;
    font-size: var(--type-body);
    font-weight: 650;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .case-note {
    max-width: 60ch;
    font-size: var(--type-small);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--mute);
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .inventory {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }

    .inventory li,
    .inventory li:nth-child(2n) {
      border-right: 0;
    }

    .inventory li:last-child {
      grid-column: auto;
      border-bottom: 0;
    }

    .lead-steps {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }

    .release li {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 2px;
    }
  }
</style>